!
! Xdefaults for an Xrtg session.
!
! Install as /root/.Xdefaults; startxrtg loads it with xrdb.
!
! Two of these are about speed rather than looks, and they matter more on
! this machine than anything in the .twmrc:
!
!   XTerm*jumpScroll     redraw once after a burst of output instead of
!                        scrolling every line.  On a display where a scroll
!                        is a blitter copy of the whole window, the
!                        difference between this on and off is the
!                        difference between usable and not.
!   XClock*update: 60    a seconds hand redraws the clock once a second,
!                        forever, on a machine where that is not free.
!                        Minutes are enough.
!
! Colours are given in full, because a 16-bit TrueColor screen has no
! colormap to run out of -- the thing that made 1990s X sessions grey.
!
! The terminal font is 8x13.  The sizes to move between, all present in
! /usr/X/lib/fonts/misc with matching bold aliases, are 6x13, 7x13, 8x13,
! 9x15 and 10x20.  At 1280x720 an 80x24 xterm in 8x13 is 640x312 pixels.

*customization:          -color

! ---- xterm ---------------------------------------------------------------

XTerm*font:              8x13
XTerm*boldFont:          8x13bold
XTerm*background:        #10161c
XTerm*foreground:        #c9d1d9
XTerm*cursorColor:       #e8913a
XTerm*pointerColor:      #e8913a
XTerm*internalBorder:    3
XTerm*scrollBar:         true
XTerm*saveLines:         500
XTerm*jumpScroll:        true
XTerm*multiScroll:       true
XTerm*scrollTtyOutput:   false
XTerm*scrollKey:         true
! The visual bell is off on purpose.  xterm implements it by XORing the
! whole window, flushing, and XORing it back -- see VisualBell() in
! xterm/misc.c -- and a read-modify-write over every pixel is the most
! expensive thing that can be asked of this card.  A read across Zorro
! cannot be posted the way a write can, so it is not the cost of a fill;
! at 80x24 it is 200 000 pixels touched twice, twice.  Hold down Tab and
! you watch it happen.  The driver now does that XOR two pixels per bus
! cycle instead of one, which halves it, but half of far too slow is
! still too slow for something that fires on every stray keystroke.
XTerm*visualBell:        false
XTerm*titeInhibit:       true
! XDM supplies no inherited login tty settings. /etc/profile sets echoe
! and erase correctly; use a login shell for newly opened terminals.
XTerm*loginShell:        true

! ---- xclock --------------------------------------------------------------

XClock*update:           60
XClock*background:       #10161c
XClock*foreground:       #c9d1d9
XClock*hands:            #e8913a
XClock*highlight:        #e8913a
XClock*borderWidth:      0

! ---- everything else -----------------------------------------------------

*Background:             #1b2129
*Foreground:             #c9d1d9
*borderColor:            #3a4451
*Font:                   -adobe-helvetica-medium-r-normal--12-*-*-*-*-*-*-*
